#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""npu-transcribe - turn audio/video files into transcripts with Whisper on the Intel NPU.

Uses openvino_genai.WhisperPipeline on the Intel NPU (Core Ultra) and falls back
to the CPU when the NPU is missing or fails. The installer runs it in its own
venv through a wrapper in ~/.local/bin. Audio is decoded by the
ffmpeg CLI straight into memory (16 kHz mono float32, no temp files) and fed to
Whisper in blocks of at most 30 s (one Whisper window) cut at the quietest moment, so
memory stays flat for multi-hour recordings.

Outputs (next to the input file unless --out is given):
  NAME.srt  subtitles, one Whisper segment per cue
  NAME.md   readable paragraphs with [hh:mm:ss] markers about every 30 s
  NAME.txt  the same paragraphs without markers (only with --format ...,txt)
Existing files are never overwritten (your own NAME.md notes, downloaded NAME.srt
subtitles, an earlier transcript): the outputs then become NAME.2.*, NAME.3.*, ...
unless --overwrite is given.

With --speakers, the voices are told apart (sherpa-onnx speaker diarization on the
CPU) and every line is labelled Speaker 1, Speaker 2, ... in the order they first speak.
--meeting DIR turns a BMeet meeting recording (DIR/part-N-mic.wav + part-N-remote.wav,
written by bmeet-meeting-audio) into a Markdown transcript: the microphone is "You",
the voices in the system audio become Person 1, Person 2, ...

Examples:
  npu-transcribe meeting.m4a
  npu-transcribe --model small --format srt,md,txt --out ~/transcripts *.mp4
  npu-transcribe --language ru --translate interview.ogg   # Russian speech -> English text
  npu-transcribe --speakers interview.m4a                    # Speaker 1: ... Speaker 2: ...
  npu-transcribe --speakers 3 panel.mp4                      # exactly three voices
  npu-transcribe --meeting DIR --output 2026-10-12_1041-meeting.md

Notes:
  * Models: turbo (default when installed) is the more accurate one and on the
    NPU about as fast as small. small needs less RAM (~2.6 vs ~3.9 GB RSS), compiles faster and is
    ~3x faster on the CPU, so it is the better choice without the NPU.
  * The first NPU run compiles the model (small ~1 min, turbo ~4 min); the result
    is cached in ~/.cache/npu-ov-cache and later loads take a few seconds.
  * --translate always produces English. whisper-large-v3-turbo was not trained
    for translation, so --translate switches to the small model unless --model
    was given explicitly.
  * --language auto detects the language once per file (first non-silent block).
    On the NPU this needs a fresh pipeline per file (~3 s reload, see
    Whisper.ready_for_detection); pass --language to skip that in big batches.
  * Models live in ~/.local/share/npu-models (env NPU_MODELS_DIR); the speaker models in
    its speaker-diarization/ folder.
  * --speakers keeps the whole file in memory (~230 MB per hour of audio) and uses
    Whisper's word timestamps, so a sentence that crosses a change of speaker is split.
  * Exit status: 0 all files done, 1 some file failed, 2 usage error, 130 Ctrl+C.
  * Every finished file (done or failed) is appended as one JSON line to
    ~/.local/share/npu-transcribe/history.jsonl (env NPU_TRANSCRIBE_HISTORY),
    which the BMeet app and panel widget list.
"""
